ACKS‑WM: Adaptive Causal Koopman–SINDy World‑Model from Sparse Sensors – Version 2


**Ouadi Maakoul**


## Abstract

Learning interpretable, causal, and stable dynamical models of spatiotemporal systems from sparse, noisy sensor data is a central challenge in data‑driven science. Recent SHRED‑type methods have shown that joint field reconstruction and equation discovery is feasible, but they lack formal guarantees on structural identifiability, long‑horizon stability, and causal structure, rely on fixed symbolic libraries, and offer no principled uncertainty quantification. This chapter presents ACKS‑WM, a unified theoretical architecture that extends the SHRED paradigm with five intertwined innovations. (i) An adaptive, stochastic minimum‑description‑length (S‑MDL) library expands only when a candidate term reduces the residual by more than the ensemble’s predictive uncertainty, preventing over‑fitting to noise. (ii) A causal invariance module, augmented by partial physical identity pinning, anchors the discovered latent causal graph to the spatial sensor locations, producing a physically interpretable directed acyclic graph. (iii) A cascaded stability certification strategy uses barrier‑focussed counterexample sampling to trigger expensive formal verification only when the Lyapunov function and Koopman invariance gap are jointly “stability‑ready,” drastically reducing computational overhead. (iv) A manifold‑freezing curriculum eliminates destructive gradient interference between the Lyapunov penalty and library expansion, guaranteeing stable training. (v) Conformal prediction over an ensemble decoder provides exact marginal coverage for one‑step forecasts and asymptotically valid coverage for multi‑step rollouts.

We prove, under explicit excitation, local‑attractor observability, and minimality conditions, that (a) the latent dynamics are identifiable up to a diffeomorphism, with a unique MDL‑optimal sparse representation within the learned coordinates; (b) a verified neural Lyapunov function yields exponential boundedness on a compact attractor; (c) the adaptive library terminates finitely with high probability, even under measurement noise; (d) with sufficiently diverse soft interventions and identity pinning, the causal graph among observed physical quantities is identifiable up to a permutation of the pinned nodes; and (e) the multi‑step Koopman forecasting error grows linearly with the invariance gap. The full training algorithm, incorporating all safeguards, is detailed. This work provides the theoretical foundation for a physically interpretable and certifiably robust next‑generation digital twin.

