## 2. Related Work

### 2.1 Sparse Sensor Reconstruction – The SHRED Ecosystem

SHRED uses a GRU encoder and shallow decoder to reconstruct fields from sensor histories. SINDy‑SHRED (Gao, Williams & Kutz, 2026; PNAS, 2026) adds a SINDy regulariser, yielding joint field reconstruction and sparse governing equation discovery. T‑SHRED replaces the GRU with a Transformer; SHRED‑ROM enables parametric reduced‑order modelling; LAPIS‑SHRED (Bao et al., 2026) introduces a modular three‑stage pipeline that reconstructs complete spatiotemporal dynamics from short temporal windows using a pre‑trained SHRED and a latent temporal sequence model; UQ‑SHRED introduces engression‑based distributional learning. None of these methods incorporate an adaptive library, formal stability certificate, or explicit causal graph recovery.

### 2.2 Equation Discovery and Adaptive Symbolic Regression

SINDy (Brunton et al., 2016) discovers sparse governing equations via ℓ₁‑regularised regression over a fixed library. SINDy‑autoencoders (Champion et al., 2019) learn latent coordinates simultaneously. Bayesian SINDy autoencoders and Ensemble‑SINDy (Fasel et al., 2022) improve robustness. Recently, AutoSINDy (Basiri & Nicholson, 2026) introduced a hybrid Discovery‑then‑Solve framework that uses PySR‑based symbolic regression to automatically generate candidate basis functions, achieving 92.8% ground‑truth recovery across canonical nonlinear systems. Desmond (2026) demonstrated that exhaustive symbolic regression with MDL model selection can directly trade off accuracy and complexity in information‑theoretic units. ACKS‑WM’s S‑MDL expansion criterion synthesises these advances: it uses an ensemble‑variance gate to distinguish genuine missing physics from noise, and the MDL penalty provides a principled complexity‑control mechanism.

### 2.3 Koopman Operator Theory and Diagnostics

Deep Koopman methods learn finite‑dimensional linear embeddings. Colbrook et al. (2024) proved that a finite invariant subspace is impossible for many systems. Conradie et al. (2026) recently introduced a posteriori diagnostics: the principal angle (invariance proximity) θ₁ between the lifted subspace and its Koopman image quantifies the irreducible error, and multi‑step forecasting error grows linearly with sin θ₁. Shah & Cortes (2026) developed a unified algebraic framework for subspace pruning via principal vectors, reducing the computational complexity of tracking principal angles by an order of magnitude. ACKS‑WM exploits these diagnostics for dictionary refinement and verification gating.

### 2.4 Causal Discovery in Dynamical Systems

CausalKinetiX (Pfister et al., 2019) recovers causal differential equations from interventional data. In latent spaces, CITRIS (Lippe et al., 2022) and Lachapelle et al. (2022) establish identifiability under soft interventions. Wendland & Kschischo (2026) recently showed that observability of latent dynamics from observed data is necessary for identifying dynamic treatment effects, linking control‑theoretic observability to causal identifiability. ACKS‑WM uses in silico additive shifts as soft interventions; its causal identifiability theorem is placed within this modern framework and strengthened by physical pinning.

### 2.5 Formal Stability via Verified Lyapunov Functions

Neural Lyapunov functions can be formally verified with δ‑complete SMT solvers (dReal). α,β‑CROWN (Zhang et al., 2025) provides orders‑of‑magnitude speedup for continuous‑time verification. Li et al. (2025) extended α,β‑CROWN with automatic Jacobian bound propagation and a novel verification scheme that avoids expensive bisection, achieving 40–10,000× speedup over dReal. Compositionally verifiable vector Lyapunov functions scale to high dimensions under weak coupling. Henzinger, Kueffner & Yu (2025) introduced dynamic runtime verification of neural certificates, performing on‑the‑fly verification over a lookahead region during deployment. ACKS‑WM adopts a triggered verification protocol that drastically reduces total computation while preserving guarantees.

### 2.6 Uncertainty Quantification with Conformal Prediction

Fasel et al. (2025) integrated conformal prediction with Ensemble‑SINDy, achieving exact marginal coverage for time‑series forecasts. For multi‑step predictions, Wang (2026) proposed AcMCP, which leverages the autocorrelation structure of multi‑step forecast errors to achieve statistically efficient prediction intervals with long‑run coverage guarantees. Lee, Xu & Xie (2026) developed flow‑based conformal prediction for multi‑dimensional time series with exact non‑asymptotic marginal coverage and finite‑sample conditional coverage bounds. ACKS‑WM grounds its UQ module in these methods.

