### A.7 Proof of Theorem 6.6 (Conformal Coverage)

**Theorem 6.6.**

(a) **One‑step coverage.** Split‑conformal prediction guarantees \(\mathbb{P}(y_{t+1} \in \hat C_{t+1}) \ge 1-\alpha\) exactly.

(b) **Multi‑step coverage.** Under \(\beta\)-mixing assumptions on the data generating process, the AcMCP method (Wang, 2026) yields asymptotically valid long‑run coverage.

(c) **Multidimensional outputs.** The flow‑based conformal predictor (Lee, Xu & Xie, 2026) provides exact non‑asymptotic marginal coverage and a finite‑sample conditional coverage bound.

*Proof.*

**(a)** This is the standard split‑conformal argument (Vovk, Gammerman & Shafer, 2005; Fasel et al., 2025). Given a calibration set \(\mathcal{D}_{\text{cal}} = \{(y_t, \hat y_t)\}_{t=1}^{N_{\text{cal}}}\) of independent prediction–outcome pairs, compute non‑conformity scores \(R_t = \|y_t - \hat y_t\|\). For a user‑specified level \(\alpha\), define the empirical quantile

\[
\hat q = \text{the } \lceil (N_{\text{cal}}+1)(1-\alpha) \rceil\text{-th smallest value of } \{R_1,\dots,R_{N_{\text{cal}}}\}.
\]

For a new test point with prediction \(\hat y_{\text{test}}\), the prediction interval \(\hat C = [\hat y_{\text{test}} \pm \hat q]\) satisfies \(\mathbb{P}(y_{\text{test}} \in \hat C) \ge 1-\alpha\) exactly, provided the calibration and test residuals are exchangeable. In the one‑step‑ahead setting with i.i.d. or exchangeable data, this condition holds.

**(b)** For multi‑step rollouts, residuals are serially dependent and not exchangeable. Wang (2026, Theorem 1) proposed the AcMCP method, which explicitly models the MA(\(h-1\)) autocorrelation structure of \(h\)-step‑ahead forecast errors under a general non‑stationary autoregressive process. Under a \(\beta\)-mixing condition (the process forgets its past at a sufficiently fast rate), the block‑conformal construction groups consecutive residuals into approximately independent blocks. The resulting prediction intervals achieve long‑run coverage approaching \(1-\alpha\) as \(T \to \infty\).

**(c)** For multi‑dimensional outputs (the full field reconstruction), Lee, Xu & Xie (2026) developed flow‑based conformal prediction. A normalising flow transforms the vector‑valued residuals into approximately independent scalar scores. Split‑conformal calibration on these transformed scores yields exact non‑asymptotic marginal coverage \(\mathbb{P}(y_{t+1} \in \hat C_{t+1}) \ge 1-\alpha\). Moreover, under smoothness conditions on the flow, a finite‑sample conditional coverage bound holds (Lee, Xu & Xie, 2026, Theorem 3.2). ∎

