### A.8 Additional Technical Background

**A.8.1 The NOTEARS acyclicity constraint.**

For a weighted adjacency matrix \(W \in \mathbb{R}^{n \times n}\), Zheng et al. (2018) proved that the function

\[
h(W) = \operatorname{tr}(e^{W \circ W}) - n
\]

satisfies \(h(W) = 0\) if and only if the directed graph encoded by \(W\) is acyclic. The function \(h\) is smooth with gradient \(\nabla h(W) = (e^{W \circ W})^T \circ 2W\), enabling gradient‑based optimisation. This transforms the combinatorial DAG constraint into a continuous equality constraint, which can be added to the ACKS‑WM training objective as \(\lambda_{\text{DAG}} h(A)\) when acyclicity of the instantaneous graph is desired.

**A.8.2 ODEs and cyclic structural causal models.**

Mooij, Janzing & Schölkopf (2013) established that the equilibrium states of a first‑order ODE system can be described by a deterministic structural causal model (SCM), and that this correspondence extends naturally to **cyclic** SCMs. In the ODE context, causal feedback loops (cycles in the instantaneous dependency graph) are a feature, not a bug—they represent genuine physical interactions such as mutual inhibition or activation. The temporal DAG obtained by unrolling the continuous‑time dynamics across discrete steps resolves any apparent conflict between cyclicity and causality. Our framework follows this ODE‑to‑SCM philosophy: the instantaneous Jacobian support may contain cycles (reflecting physical feedback), while the temporal causal graph is guaranteed acyclic by the arrow of time.

